Title: SCSS Responsive Design & Media Query Standards

Purpose: Define clear rules for media queries, breakpoint usage, and responsive patterns to ensure consistent, maintainable responsive behavior across all components and layouts.

Sections:

1. Breakpoint Philosophy
   - Use the semantic media query mixins defined in `src/scss/utils/_mixins.scss` (lines 218-252). NEVER write inline `@media` queries outside of mixin definitions.
   - Current breakpoint strategy is hybrid: orientation-aware for tablets, max-width for general responsiveness.
   - Breakpoints (from largest to smallest):
       * `tablet-horizontal-large`: max-width 1367px (desktop → large tablet)
       * `tablet-horizontal`: landscape + max-width 1181px (landscape tablet)
       * `tablet-vertical`: portrait + max-width 1025px (portrait tablet)
       * `smartphone-horizontal`: landscape + max-height 410px (landscape phone)
       * `smartphone-vertical`: max-width 600px (portrait phone)
       * `smartphone-small`: portrait + max-height 700px (small phone)
   - Rationale: Orientation-based queries ensure layouts adapt to device usage patterns, not just screen width.

2. Mixin Usage Rules
   - ALWAYS use semantic mixin names, NEVER raw media queries in component/layout files.
   - Apply mixins at the appropriate selector nesting level to minimize specificity conflicts.
   - Order responsive overrides from largest to smallest breakpoint for clarity.

   Good example from `_purpose.scss`:
   ```scss
   h2 {
       font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3rem);

       @include tablet-horizontal {
           margin-left: 5%;
       }

       @include tablet-vertical {
           margin-left: 5%;
       }
   }
   ```

   Bad example (inline media query):
   ```scss
   .navbar {
       @media (max-width: 760px) {  // ❌ Use mixin instead
           ul { flex-direction: column; }
       }
   }
   ```

3. DRY Responsive Patterns
   - When multiple breakpoints require identical adjustments, create a shared mixin for that adjustment or consolidate into a single breakpoint.
   - Avoid duplicating the same property changes across multiple breakpoints.

   Before (duplicated logic):
   ```scss
   p {
       width: 75ch;

       @include tablet-horizontal {
           width: 90%;
           margin-left: 5%;
       }

       @include tablet-vertical {
           width: 90%;
           margin-left: 5%;
       }
   }
   ```

   After (consolidated):
   ```scss
   @mixin tablet-adjustments {
       width: 90%;
       margin-left: 5%;
   }

   p {
       width: 75ch;

       @include tablet-horizontal {
           @include tablet-adjustments;
       }

       @include tablet-vertical {
           @include tablet-adjustments;
       }
   }
   ```

   Or if the adjustment is truly identical for all tablet sizes, consider creating a new `@mixin tablet-all` in `_mixins.scss`:
   ```scss
   @mixin tablet-all {
       @media (max-width: 1367px) {
           @content;
       }
   }
   ```

4. Fluid Typography & Spacing
   - Prefer `clamp()` for font sizes: `font-size: clamp(min, preferred, max)` to reduce media query count.
   - Use viewport units (vw, vh) within `clamp()` for the middle value to create smooth scaling.
   - Example: `font-size: clamp(1rem, 2vw + 0.5rem, 2rem)` scales from 1rem to 2rem based on viewport.
   - Use the `$spacing-scale` map from `_mixins.scss` for consistent spacing at all breakpoints.

   Good pattern:
   ```scss
   h1 {
       font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 4rem);
       @include spacing(padding, lg);  // Consistent spacing token
   }
   ```

5. Layout Adaptation Strategy
   - Desktop → Tablet: Reduce margins/padding, adjust grid columns, maintain visual hierarchy.
   - Tablet → Mobile: Switch from multi-column to single-column, stack flex items, increase tap target sizes.
   - Always test orientation changes; horizontal mobile has unique constraints (max-height).

   Responsive grid example:
   ```scss
   .cards-grid {
       display: grid;
       grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr));
       gap: 2rem;

       @include tablet-vertical {
           grid-template-columns: 1fr;  // Single column on portrait tablet
           gap: 1rem;
       }

       @include smartphone-vertical {
           padding: 0 1rem;  // Add horizontal padding on mobile
       }
   }
   ```

6. Component-Level Responsive Rules
   - Components should be responsive by default using fluid units (%, rem, ch, vw) before adding breakpoints.
   - Only add media query mixins when fluid approaches can't solve the layout need.
   - Test components in isolation at all breakpoints before integrating into layouts.

   Prefer fluid:
   ```scss
   .button {
       padding: 0.75rem 2rem;
       font-size: clamp(0.875rem, 2vw, 1rem);
       // No media queries needed
   }
   ```

   Add breakpoint when necessary:
   ```scss
   .button-group {
       display: flex;
       gap: 1rem;

       @include smartphone-vertical {
           flex-direction: column;  // Stack vertically on small screens
       }
   }
   ```

7. Refactoring Inline Media Queries
   - Any `@media` found outside `_mixins.scss` is a refactoring candidate.
   - Convert inline queries to semantic mixins or consolidate into existing mixins.

   Found in `_variables.scss` (lines 109-113):
   ```scss
   .navbar {
       @media (max-width: $breakpoint-mobile) {
           ul { flex-direction: column; }
       }
   }
   ```

   Refactor to:
   ```scss
   .navbar {
       @include smartphone-vertical {
           ul { flex-direction: column; }
       }
   }
   ```

8. Testing Responsive Behavior
   - Test at all defined breakpoints plus intermediate sizes.
   - Critical test points: 1367px, 1181px, 1025px, 600px, and orientation changes at each.
   - Use browser DevTools device emulation for realistic testing (iPhone SE, iPad, desktop).
   - Verify `clamp()` scales smoothly by resizing viewport gradually.

9. Performance & CSS Output
   - Media query mixins group repeated breakpoints in compiled CSS when using `@use`, reducing file size.
   - Avoid nesting media queries beyond 2 levels to prevent specificity bloat.
   - Prefer `@content` in mixins over parameterized overrides for flexibility.

   Efficient pattern:
   ```scss
   .component {
       property: value;

       @include tablet-horizontal {
           property: adjusted-value;  // Clean, single-level nesting
       }
   }
   ```

   Avoid:
   ```scss
   .component {
       @include tablet-horizontal {
           .child {
               @include smartphone-vertical {  // ❌ Too deeply nested
                   property: value;
               }
           }
       }
   }
   ```

10. Adding New Breakpoints
    - Before adding a new breakpoint mixin, verify no existing mixin covers the use case.
    - New mixins must go in `src/scss/utils/_mixins.scss` with clear semantic names.
    - Document the breakpoint in this file and in `_mixins.scss` comments.
    - Update all relevant layout files to use the new mixin consistently.

    Template for new breakpoint:
    ```scss
    @mixin desktop-large {
        @media (min-width: 1920px) {
            @content;
        }
    }
    ```

11. Common Responsive Patterns (Reference)
    - Hide/show elements: Use `display: none` inside breakpoint mixins, not separate classes.
    - Navigation toggle: Switch from horizontal to hamburger menu at `@include tablet-vertical`.
    - Card grids: Use `repeat(auto-fit, minmax(X, 1fr))` with breakpoint-specific `minmax` values.
    - Hero sections: Maintain min-height at all breakpoints, adjust padding/font-size.
    - Footer: Keep fixed height via `--footer-height` CSS var, adjust only padding/gap.

Use this file alongside `scss-patterns.mdc` and `scss-layouts.mdc` to maintain consistent responsive behavior.
